  "locator": "page 16",
  "segments": [
    {
      "segment_id": "S00001",
      "start_char": 0,
      "end_char": 159,
      "text": "MEXICO\nBox 2. Global Tightening and Inflation Scenario\nA global tightening and inflation scenario was simulated with the IMF’s G20MOD multi-country DSGE model\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00002",
      "start_char": 159,
      "end_char": 391,
      "text": "to better understand the consequences of multiple downside shocks on the Mexican economy.1 The model\nresults are presented in cumulative layers, with each successive layer adding another shock on top of those\nin the previous layer.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00003",
      "start_char": 391,
      "end_char": 611,
      "text": "An initial layer simulates the direct effect of a temporary shock to global oil and food prices, which would\nrise by 10 and 50 percent, respectively, relative to the baseline. The shock would raise headline inflation in\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00004",
      "start_char": 611,
      "end_char": 829,
      "text": "Mexico by nearly 1 percent above an already elevated baseline in year-on-year terms for a few quarters, but\nit would only have limited direct effects on output in the absence of a significant monetary policy response.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00005",
      "start_char": 829,
      "end_char": 1050,
      "text": "In a second layer, the commodity shock on top of the baseline, which already includes inflation at a 20-year\nhigh, causes a temporary dislocation of inflation expectations globally, including in Mexico. This results in a\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00006",
      "start_char": 1050,
      "end_char": 1267,
      "text": "sharper increase in the domestic (and global) policy interest rate, in line with the increase in inflation\nexpectations, to bring expectations back in line with the inflation target and limit second-round effects. In\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00007",
      "start_char": 1267,
      "end_char": 1485,
      "text": "this case, output levels fall substantially below baseline, peaking at more than 1 percent below in the first\nhalf of 2023. Higher policy rates also present elevated systemic liquidity risk, discussed in detail in the\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00008",
      "start_char": 1485,
      "end_char": 1662,
      "text": "accompanying 2022 Financial System Stability Assessment (FSSA) for Mexico.\nLast, a third layer us added with a 1 percent decline in domestic demand, inspired by diminished real\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00009",
      "start_char": 1662,
      "end_char": 1866,
      "text": "earnings expectations, or perhaps an un-modeled credit contraction. This brings output to more than\n2percent below baseline at the trough while somewhat attenuating prices pressures. The three layers put\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00010",
      "start_char": 1866,
      "end_char": 2097,
      "text": "substantial cumulative pressure on the corporate interest rate though a capital flow shock is not modeled\ndirectly.\n1 The simulation is consistent with the global downside scenario presented in the July 2022 World Economic Outlook\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00011",
      "start_char": 2097,
      "end_char": 2313,
      "text": "Update. It was constructed on the basis of shocks to economic variables relative from their baseline levels and, as such,\nthe results generally are not sensitive to the baseline levels.\n12 INTERNATIONAL MONETARY FUND"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "schema_version": "locator_index_v1",
  "source_id": "MAC-010",
  "source_sha256": "8fac4146590dbe2ed458f7356748f5880d021e03cc4982b1e7efe083d33f622a",
  "locator": "page 17",
  "segments": [
    {
      "segment_id": "S00001",
      "start_char": 0,
      "end_char": 159,
      "text": "MEXICO\nPOLICY DISCUSSIONS\nA. Tackling High Inflation\nMonetary Policy\n19. Banxico has taken a proactive approach to address increasingly broad-based inflation.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00002",
      "start_char": 159,
      "end_char": 358,
      "text": "Since June 2021, successive and gradually larger increases in the reference rate brought the ex ante\nreal policy rate (i.e., the nominal rate adjusted for one-year ahead inflation expectations) to a\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00003",
      "start_char": 358,
      "end_char": 556,
      "text": "moderately restrictive level by September 2022 at about 4 percent.6 This has been an appropriately\ncalibrated response to the upward surprises to inflation and sequential delays in returning to the\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00004",
      "start_char": 556,
      "end_char": 764,
      "text": "inflation target. In recent monetary policy statements, Banxico has also rightly indicated its intention\nto further increase the policy rate. It is expected that a policy rate of at least 10 percent would be\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00005",
      "start_char": 764,
      "end_char": 988,
      "text": "required for inflation to reverse close to the central bank’s 3-percent target within the usual two\nyears or so.\nInflation and Expectations\nReal Ex-Ante Policy Rates\n(Seasonally adjusted, y/y)\n9 (Percent)\n10\nBrazil\nHeadline\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00006",
      "start_char": 988,
      "end_char": 1181,
      "text": "8 Inflation 8 Chile\nMexico\n7 Core 6 Peru\nInflation\nColombia\n4\n6 Expectations\n: 12 Months 2\n5\nExpectations\n0\n: 1-4 Years\n4 -2\nExpectations\n: 5-8 Years\n3 -4\nSources: Banxico and Haver Analytics.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00007",
      "start_char": 1181,
      "end_char": 1373,
      "text": "20. Inflation risks are expected to remain heightened for some time. The proactive policy\ntightening already put in place, alongside further increases in the policy rate, and weakening global\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00008",
      "start_char": 1373,
      "end_char": 1573,
      "text": "demand are expected to lead to a slowdown in activity and a decline in inflation. However, there is\nsignificant uncertainty about the timing, speed, and durability of the downward path for inflation.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00009",
      "start_char": 1573,
      "end_char": 1778,
      "text": "This uncertainty arises from the possibility of: (i) further shocks, including spillovers to local raw and\nprocessed food prices from global commodity prices and domestic food prices, especially resulting\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00010",
      "start_char": 1778,
      "end_char": 1973,
      "text": "from severe drought conditions in some Mexican states; (ii) if inflation imported from advanced\neconomies is more persistent than expected; (iii) longer-lasting price pressures from supply chain\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00011",
      "start_char": 1973,
      "end_char": 2160,
      "text": "6 In its April–June 2019 Quarterly Report, Banxico used a range of models to establish a range between 1.8 and 3.4\npercent for the neutral real policy rate.\nINTERNATIONAL MONETARY FUND 13"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "schema_version": "locator_index_v1",
  "source_id": "MAC-010",
  "source_sha256": "8fac4146590dbe2ed458f7356748f5880d021e03cc4982b1e7efe083d33f622a",
  "locator": "page 18",
  "segments": [
    {
      "segment_id": "S00001",
      "start_char": 0,
      "end_char": 206,
      "text": "MEXICO\nconstraints; (iv) further expected increases in the minimum wage feeding into costs and prices;\n(v) inertia resulting from more frequent domestic price adjustments; and (vi) further upticks in near-\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00002",
      "start_char": 206,
      "end_char": 406,
      "text": "term inflation expectations that then feed in to wages and prices. The inflation surge in Mexico\nreflects both shocks in standard determinants (such as relative food and import prices, falling slack,\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00003",
      "start_char": 406,
      "end_char": 626,
      "text": "and rising inflation expectations), but also a series of positive residual factors, and preliminary signs\nof greater inertia (Annex IX).\nInflation Expectations—1-4 Years Ahead Mexico Processed Food and U.S. Overall Food\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00004",
      "start_char": 626,
      "end_char": 835,
      "text": "(Central Bank survey responses) Inflation\n6.0 0.5 14\nMex - Core Food\n12\n0.4 U.S. - Food & Bev\n5.0\n10\n0.3\n8\n4.0\n0.2 6\n3.0 0.1 4\n2\n2.0 0.0\n0\n-2\nInterquartile Range Total Range\nAverage Interquartile Spread (RHS)\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00005",
      "start_char": 835,
      "end_char": 1066,
      "text": "Sources: Haver Analytics and Banxico.\n21. Risk considerations call for further increases in the policy rate and maintaining it at\nthat higher level. The risks to inflation are not only high but also asymmetric in the usual one-to-\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00006",
      "start_char": 1066,
      "end_char": 1257,
      "text": "two-year monetary policy horizon: upside surprises to inflation seem more likely than downside\nshocks. One contributing factor is backward-looking elements in the inflation process in Mexico\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00007",
      "start_char": 1257,
      "end_char": 1455,
      "text": "according to Philips curve estimates for Mexico (Annex IX), which suggests additional risks from\nrecent inflation shocks. Given these asymmetric risks and the substantial costs of de-anchoring wage\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00008",
      "start_char": 1455,
      "end_char": 1648,
      "text": "and price formation, a risk management approach would argue for policy rates to rise further and\nstay firmly restrictive for some time, so as to keep wage and price formation anchored. Such an\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00009",
      "start_char": 1648,
      "end_char": 1834,
      "text": "approach would entail a real ex ante policy rate that would peak at over 5 percent in 2023, broadly\nconsistent with market expectations for nominal rates and staff’s inflation forecast.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00010",
      "start_char": 1834,
      "end_char": 2016,
      "text": "22. Clear monetary policy communication will increase policy effectiveness during this\nperiod of significant inflation uncertainty. Banxico has appropriately taken steps to help the\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00011",
      "start_char": 2016,
      "end_char": 2218,
      "text": "public better understand its policy decisions in this context. Steps have included the publication of\nupdated inflation forecasts with every rate decision since August 2021 and, more recently, guidance\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00012",
      "start_char": 2218,
      "end_char": 2416,
      "text": "on the direction of the next policy rate change in the monetary policy statement. These efforts have\nhelped the public better understand the Governing Board’s policy decision. To further strengthen\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00013",
      "start_char": 2416,
      "end_char": 2613,
      "text": "this understanding, Banxico could begin publishing more information on the policy rate path that\nunderpins its macro forecast, including the expected rate and length of its stay at the peak of the\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00014",
      "start_char": 2613,
      "end_char": 2808,
      "text": "tightening cycle, and possible triggers for changes in policies. If clearly presented as expectations\nand not a policy commitment, changes in the expected policy rate path would provide valuable\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00015",
      "start_char": 2808,
      "end_char": 3028,
      "text": "information on how the central bank expects to adjust policy as economic conditions change. A\nbroader review of the experience with Banxico’s inflation targeting framework over the past two\n14 INTERNATIONAL MONETARY FUND"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "schema_version": "locator_index_v1",
  "source_id": "MAC-010",
  "source_sha256": "8fac4146590dbe2ed458f7356748f5880d021e03cc4982b1e7efe083d33f622a",
  "locator": "page 19",
  "segments": [
    {
      "segment_id": "S00001",
      "start_char": 0,
      "end_char": 237,
      "text": "MEXICO\ndecades could provide useful suggestions for further improvements to the policy framework and the\ncommunications toolkit.\nFiscal Measures\n23. The authorities have used largely untargeted subsidies to mitigate the rise in the cost\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00002",
      "start_char": 237,
      "end_char": 428,
      "text": "of living. The authorities expect that these subsidies will cost around 2 percent of GDP (see Annex\nVI for a description of the authorities’ anti-inflationary fiscal measures) which include:\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00003",
      "start_char": 428,
      "end_char": 616,
      "text": "• Retail fuel price stabilization has directly reduced cost pressures, lowering inflation by an\nestimated 2 percentage points this year. The measure has come at a sizeable budgetary cost,\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00004",
      "start_char": 616,
      "end_char": 831,
      "text": "estimated at around 1.5 percent of GDP, and has disproportionately benefited higher income\nhouseholds. Furthermore, by diluting price signals, the policy has short-circuited the desirable\nadjustment in fuel demand.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00005",
      "start_char": 831,
      "end_char": 1014,
      "text": "• The measures to mitigate the impact of higher food prices have come at a smaller budgetary\ncost. They have mostly relied on preexisting programs (e.g., increased emphasis on direct\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00006",
      "start_char": 1014,
      "end_char": 1235,
      "text": "fertilizer provision to small farmers or support for subsistence farming), and their direct impact\non inflation has been minimal.\n24. Overall, these fiscal and preexisting redistributive measures have helped support real\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00007",
      "start_char": 1235,
      "end_char": 1425,
      "text": "incomes. The higher food and energy prices have only increased the deficit by around ¼ percent of\nGDP with fuel subsidies offset by higher oil revenues and food-related measures funded from\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00008",
      "start_char": 1425,
      "end_char": 1622,
      "text": "reducing other expenditures. However, in the event of a renewed increase in energy prices, the fiscal\neffects would depend on the level of refining margins (“crack spread”) and the duration of the\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00009",
      "start_char": 1622,
      "end_char": 1820,
      "text": "higher global prices. In addition to these efforts to lessen the increase in the cost of living, the\nadministration has increased the universal social (noncontributory) pensions and is projected to\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00010",
      "start_char": 1820,
      "end_char": 2015,
      "text": "raise the minimum wage from 42 percent of the median formal sector wage in 2018 to 59 percent in\n2022). Overall, in the context of a stronger economic recovery and record-high remittance inflow,\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00011",
      "start_char": 2015,
      "end_char": 2200,
      "text": "these fiscal efforts, the higher minimum wage, and other labor market measures may have\ncontributed to an increase in real per capita labor income of about 5 percent (y/y) by mid-2022.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00012",
      "start_char": 2200,
      "end_char": 2379,
      "text": "25. The proposed neutral fiscal stance in 2023 strikes a balance between supporting\nmonetary policy in its disinflation efforts while avoiding a material fiscal drag on activity.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00013",
      "start_char": 2379,
      "end_char": 2580,
      "text": "With the economy currently operating at close to potential the priority is to restore low and stable\ninflation. A restrictive monetary stance and a broadly neutral fiscal stance provide an appropriate\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00014",
      "start_char": 2580,
      "end_char": 2777,
      "text": "policy mix to achieve this goal. However, continuing large minimum wage increases will likely add to\ninflation pressures, working at counter purpose to monetary and fiscal policies and potentially\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00015",
      "start_char": 2777,
      "end_char": 2949,
      "text": "reducing formal employment for lower income workers (see below).\nAuthorities’ Views\n26. The authorities agreed with the need for a robust monetary policy to keep inflation\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00016",
      "start_char": 2949,
      "end_char": 3069,
      "text": "expectations well-anchored. The early and decisive monetary policy response to the global\nINTERNATIONAL MONETARY FUND 15"
    }
  ]
}
